DATABASE_URL=postgres://peak:peak@localhost:5432/peak
# Where replica-tolerant expression-card and delivery-log reads go. Leave it unset to make
# db.read share the primary's own pool instead of opening a second connection.
DATABASE_READ_URL=postgres://peak:peak@localhost:5433/peak
PORT=3000
# Local Supabase stack (bun run supabase:start). Both values are the CLI defaults printed by `supabase status`.
# SUPABASE_URL is where the API fetches the JWT signing keys (/auth/v1/.well-known/jwks.json);
# SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET verifies legacy HS256 tokens.
SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=super-secret-jwt-token-with-at-least-32-characters-long

# The simulated push sink (apps/api/src/push/simulated.ts) is what every measured number in
# README.md's table was produced against, and these three values are the experiment itself:
# changing them makes a run a different experiment rather than a faster one.
# The process that READS them is whichever one sends: the workers (bun run dev:worker) under
# LOAD_MODE=queue, or the scheduler itself under SCHEDULER_MODE=naive, which is the line the load
# harness prints for LOAD_MODE=naive. The harness itself reads none of them: it measures what a
# send cost from the deliveries rows the sender wrote, reads the record of these values the sender
# put on each row (deliveries.sender), and every load/results/*.json holds both beside the pinned
# values above, so a run whose sender used other settings shows up as a missed verdict, however
# small the difference. design.md "The push sink" owns the reasoning.
PUSH_SIM_LATENCY_MIN_MS=50
PUSH_SIM_LATENCY_MAX_MS=150
PUSH_SIM_FAILURE_RATE=0

# The scheduler process (bun run dev:scheduler). SCHEDULER_NOW fixes the instant a tick treats as
# the current time; it exists because the seed's target date is a fixed future date, so a measured
# run needs it and a deployment leaves it unset.
# SCHEDULER_MODE picks the tick: enqueue (the default) inserts one jobs row per due reminder for
# the workers to send; naive is the M1 inline send, kept so the M1 row stays reproducible.
# SCHEDULER_MODE=enqueue
# SCHEDULER_INTERVAL_MS=60000
# SCHEDULER_NOW=2026-09-15T12:00:00Z

# The worker processes (bun run dev:worker, one per terminal, as many as the run wants). Each
# claims WORKER_BATCH_SIZE jobs with FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED, sends them concurrently, and sleeps
# WORKER_POLL_MS when it finds none. A batch left locked by a killed worker is reclaimed by any
# worker after WORKER_LEASE_MS. A failed send is retried WORKER_BACKOFF_BASE_MS x 2^(attempts-1)
# later until WORKER_MAX_ATTEMPTS failures dead-letter it. design.md "Queue and workers (M2)".
# WORKER_BATCH_SIZE=25
# WORKER_POLL_MS=250
# WORKER_LEASE_MS=30000
# WORKER_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
# WORKER_BACKOFF_BASE_MS=1000

# The cards cache (apps/api/src/cards/cache.ts), read by the worker and the API. The worker reads
# the day's cards before every send and GET /cards/today serves them through the same module; an
# entry is served without a query while younger than CARDS_CACHE_FRESH_MS, served as it is with one
# revalidation behind it while younger than CARDS_CACHE_STALE_MS (both are ages since the entry was
# loaded), and loaded again from that age on, with at most CARDS_CACHE_MAX_ENTRIES local dates
# held. CARDS_CACHE=off makes the module a pass-through — every read is a query — which is what
# M3's control run measures. design.md "The cards cache".
# CARDS_CACHE=on
# CARDS_CACHE_FRESH_MS=60000
# CARDS_CACHE_STALE_MS=600000
# CARDS_CACHE_MAX_ENTRIES=64

# The load harness (bun run load:m1, load:m2, load:m2:restart). LOAD_MODE picks the sender the run
# measures — naive is the M1 send (the scheduler under SCHEDULER_MODE=naive), queue the enqueue
# tick plus N workers — and the three root scripts set it inline, so a copied file needs neither
# of the two lines below. LOAD_WORKER_RESTART=1 turns a queue run into the restart run: the harness
# kills one worker with SIGKILL mid-fan-out and reads what became of its batch, which is the
# "jobs lost across worker restart" column.
# It refuses a non-loopback API_URL, and it signs its own pool's tokens with SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET
# above, so the API has to be running with the same value.
# LOAD_MODE=queue
# LOAD_WORKER_RESTART=1
# API_URL=http://localhost:3000
# LOAD_POOL_USERS=200
# LOAD_REQUESTS_PER_SECOND=20
# The two refusals that keep a stuck run from waiting forever: how long the harness waits for the
# sender's first delivery, and how long the fan-out may make no progress before it gives up. In a
# restart run the stall timeout has to exceed the workers' lease, or the killed worker's batch
# waiting out its lease looks like a stall; the harness refuses a restart run at or below the pinned
# default of 30000 ms, and it cannot see a WORKER_LEASE_MS the workers were started with.
# LOAD_START_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
# LOAD_STALL_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
